      "The Black Queens started the game brightly and took an early lead, raising hopes of a place in the semi-finals. However, Malawi fought back strongly and eventually completed a comeback to secure a historic victory.\n\nGhana took the lead in the seventh minute through Chantelle Boye-Hlorkah, who finished well inside the box to put the Black Queens ahead.",
      "Malawi responded almost immediately, with Temwa Chawinga restoring parity in the 12th minute. The equaliser gave the Scorchers renewed confidence as both sides battled for control of the game.\n\nThe first half ended 1–1, leaving everything to play for after the break.",
      "Ghana pushed forward in search of a second goal but struggled to convert their opportunities. Malawi remained dangerous on the counter and continued to put pressure on the Ghanaian defence.\n\nTheir persistence eventually paid off in the 79th minute when Rose Kadzere found the back of the net to give Malawi a 2–1 lead.\n\nThe Black Queens attempted to find a late equaliser but were unable to break through Malawi’s defence before the final whistle.\n\nHistoric achievement for Malawi\n\nThe victory marks a significant moment in Malawi’s women’s football history. The Scorchers have reached the WAFCON semi-finals for the first time and have also secured a historic place at the FIFA Women’s World Cup.\n\nFor Ghana, the defeat brings an end to the team’s hopes of reaching the WAFCON semi-finals and represents a disappointing conclusion to their campaign.",
